      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "Find the formula for the $n$th term of the following geometric sequence.\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3},\\,3,\\,\\frac{27}{2},\\,…\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}$ and the second term is ${a}_{2}=3,$ so we can find the common ratio, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{3}{(\\frac{2}{3})}=\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the common ratio is $r=\\frac{9}{2},$  we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {(\\frac{9}{2})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
